"Internal Error ()\nciteturn14view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.math.iitb.ac.in/~srg/preprints/BeelenGhorpade_DiscMath2020.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.math.iitb.ac.in/~srg/preprints/BeelenGhorpade_DiscMath2020.pdf: TimeoutError--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn14view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ajc.maths.uq.edu.au/pdf/81/ajc_v81_p001.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://ajc.maths.uq.edu.au/pdf/81/ajc_v81_p001.pdf: Failed to fetch restricted URL--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v25i1p26/pdf/)\nciteturn14view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 21; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v25i1p26/pdf/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1600\nL0@P0: The Z-polynomial of a matroid\nL1@P0: Nicholas Proudfoot∗ Yuan Xu† Ben Young\nL2@P0: Department of Mathematics\nL3@P0: University of Oregon\nL4@P0: Eugene, Oregon, U.S.A.\nL5@P0: {njp, yuan, bjy}@uoregon.edu\nL6@P0: Submitted: Jun 18, 2017; Accepted: Jan 12, 2018; Published: Feb 16, 2018\nL7@P0: Mathematics Subject Classifications: 05B35, 55N33, 11B83, 12D10\nL8@P0: Abstract\nL9@P0: We introduce the Z-polynomial of a matroid, which we define in terms of the\nL10@P0: Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomial. We then exploit a symmetry of the Z-polynomial to\nL11@P0: derive a new recursion for Kazhdan-Lusztig coefficients. We solve this recursion,\nL12@P0: obtaining a closed formula for Kazhdan-Lusztig coefficients as alternating sums of\nL13@P0: multi-indexed Whitney numbers. For realizable matroids, we give a cohomological\nL14@P0: interpretation of the Z-polynomial in which the symmetry is a manifestation of\nL15@P0: Poincar´e duality.\nL16@P0: 1 Introduction\nL17@P0: The Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomial PM(t) of a matroid M was introduced by Elias, Wake\u0002field, and the first author in [EPW16]. This invariant has shown itself to be surprisingly\nL18@P0: rich, with many beautiful properties (most of them still conjectural). For example, the\nL19@P0: coefficients of PM(t) are conjecturally non-negative; in the case where M is realizable, this\nL20@P0: is proved by interpreting the coefficients as intersection cohomology Betti numbers of the\nL21@P0: reciprocal plane of the realization [EPW16, Theorem 3.10]. The polynomial PM(t) is con\u0002jecturally log concave [EPW16, Conjecture 2.5] and, even stronger, real rooted [GPY17b,\nL22@P0: Conjecture 3.2]. Furthermore, if M0is obtained from M by contracting a single element,\nL23@P0: the roots of PM0(t) are conjectured to interlace with those of PM(t) [GPY17b, Remark\nL24@P0: 3.5].\nL25@P0: If the matroid M has a finite symmetry group Γ, then one can study the equivariant\nL26@P0: Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomial P\nL27@P0: Γ\nL28@P0: M(t) [GPY17a], whose coefficients are virtual representa\u0002tions of Γ with dimension equal to the coefficients of PM(t). In the case where M is\nL29@P0: ∗Supported by NSF grant DMS-1565036.\nL30@P0: †Supported by NSF grant DMS-1510296.\nL31@P0-1: the electronic journal of combinatorics 25(1) (2018), #P1.26 1equivariantly realizable over the complex numbers, the same cohomological interpretation\nL32@P1: allows us to prove that the coefficients are honest representations [GPY17a, Corollary\nL33@P1: 2.12]. The equivariant polynomial P\nL34@P1: Γ\nL35@P1: M(t) is conjectured to be equivariantly log concave\nL36@P1: [GPY17a, Conjecture 5.3(2)].\nL37@P1: Despite all of the surprising structure that these polynomials are conjectured to\nL38@P1: have, very few examples are completely understood. Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials of\nL39@P1: thagomizer matroids coincide with Dyck path polynomials [Ged17, Theorem 1.1(1)], and\nL40@P1: Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials of fan matroids conjecturally coincide with Motzkin poly\u0002nomials [Ged]. The equivariant Kazhdan-Lusztig coefficients of uniform matroids have\nL41@P1: been computed explicitly [GPY17a, Theorem 3.1] and shown to admit the structure of\nL42@P1: finitely generated FI-modules. In contrast, the equivariant Kazhdan-Lusztig coefficients\nL43@P1: of braid matroids admit the structure of finitely generated FSop-modules [PY17, Theorem\nL44@P1: 6.1], and no explicit formula has appeared. Indeed, the problem of computing Kazhdan\u0002Lusztig coefficients of braid matroids was the main motivation for this work.\nL45@P1: In this paper we introduce the Z-polynomial ZM(t), which is defined as a weighted sum\nL46@P1: of the Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials of all possible contractions of M. The Z-polynomial\nL47@P1: is palindromic (Proposition 2.3), reflecting the fact that, when M is realizable, the co\u0002efficients of ZM(t) may be interpreted as intersection cohomology Betti numbers of a\nL48@P1: projective variety (Theorem 7.2), for which Poincar´e duality holds.\nL49@P1: Surprisingly, this symmetry of the Z-polynomial translates into a recursive formula\nL50@P1: for Kazhdan-Lusztig coefficients that is different from any of the recursive formulas seen\nL51@P1: before (Corollary 3.2). In particular, it yields a method for computing Kazhdan-Lusztig\nL52@P1: coefficients of braid matroids that is much faster than any previously available approach.\nL53@P1: Furthermore, we are able to use this recursion to obtain a formula that expresses each\nL54@P1: Kazhdan-Lusztig coefficient of M as a finite alternating sum of multi-indexed Whitney\nL55@P1: numbers (Theorem 3.3). In the case of braid matroids, this becomes a finite alternating\nL56@P1: sum of products of Stirling numbers of the second kind (Corollary 4.5). We also obtain\nL57@P1: an equivariant version of our formula (Theorem 6.1), which takes a particularly nice form\nL58@P1: for uniform matroids (Proposition 6.3).\nL59@P1: Our Theorem 3.3 bears a close resemblance to a recent result of Wakefield [Wak, The\u0002orem 5.1], who also obtained a formula for Kazhdan-Lusztig coefficients as alternating\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChen.dvi (https://www.acadsci.fi/mathematica/Vol43/vol43pp0171-0185.pdf)\nciteturn14view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 15; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.acadsci.fi/mathematica/Vol43/vol43pp0171-0185.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1425\nL0@P0: Annales Academiæ Scientiarum Fennicæ\nL1@P0: Mathematica\nL2@P0: Volumen 43, 2018, 171–185\nL3@P0: PROJECTIONS IN VECTOR\nL4@P0: SPACES OVER FINITE FIELDS\nL5@P0: Changhao Chen\nL6@P0: The University of New South Wales, School of Mathematics and Statistics\nL7@P0: Sydney NSW 2052, Australia; changhao.chenm@gmail.com\nL8@P0: Abstract. We study the projections in vector spaces over finite fields. We prove finite fields\nL9@P0: analogues of the bounds on the dimensions of the exceptional sets for Euclidean projection mapping.\nL10@P0: We provide examples which do not have exceptional projections via projections of random sets. In\nL11@P0: the end we study the projections of sets which have the (discrete) Fourier decay.\nL12@P0: 1. Introduction\nL13@P0: A fundamental problem in fractal geometry is that how the projections affect\nL14@P0: dimension. Recall the classical Marstrand–Mattila projection theorem: Let E ⊂ Rn,\nL15@P0: n ≥ 2, be a Borel set with Hausdorff dimension s.\nL16@P0: • If s ≤ m, then the orthogonal projection of E onto almost all m-dimensional\nL17@P0: subspaces has Hausdorff dimension s.\nL18@P0: • If s > m, then the orthogonal of E onto almost all m-dimensional subspaces\nL19@P0: has positive m-dimensional Lebesgue measure.\nL20@P0: In 1954 Marstrand [14] proved this projection theorem in the plane. In 1975 Mattila\nL21@P0: [15] proved this for general dimension via 1968 Kaufman’s [12] potential theoretic\nL22@P0: methods. Furthermore the bounds on the dimensions of the exceptional sets of pro\u0002jections was well studied. We put these results in the following form Theorem 1.1\nL23@P0: which come from a recent survey paper of Falconer, Fraser and Jin [8], and Mattila\nL24@P0: [17, Corollary 5.12]. Theorem 1.1 (a) was proved by Kaufman [12] for m = 1, n = 2,\nL25@P0: and by Mattila [15] for general 1 ≤ m < n. The estimate (b) was proved by Falconer\nL26@P0: [7] and all known proofs depend on Fourier transform. The estimate (c) was proved\nL27@P0: by Peres and Schlag [24] under their generalized projections.\nL28@P0: Theorem 1.1. (Bounds on the dimensions of the exceptional sets) Let E ⊂ Rn\nL29@P0: be a Borel set and s = dim E.\nL30@P0: (a) If s ≤ m and t ∈ (0, s], then\nL31@P0: dim{V ∈ G(n, m): dim πV (E) < t} ≤ m(n − m) − (m − t).\nL32@P0: (b) If s > m, then\nL33@P0: dim{V ∈ G(n, m): Hm(πV (E)) = 0} ≤ m(n − m) − (s − m).\nL34@P0: (c) If s > 2m, then\nL35@P0: dim{V ∈ G(n, m): πV (E) has empty interior } ≤ m(n − m) − (s − 2m).\nL36@P0: Here G(n, m), called Grassmanian manifold, denotes the collection of all the\nL37@P0: m-dimensional linear subspaces of Rn. The notation dim E denotes the Hausdorff\nL38@P0: dimension of the set E, and πV : Rn → V stands for the orthogonal projections onto\nL39@P0: https://doi.org/10.5186/aasfm.2018.4307\nL40@P0: 2010 Mathematics Subject Classification: Primary 05B25, 52C99.\nL41@P0-1: Key words: Exceptional sets of projections, finite fields, random subsets, Fourier transform.172 Changhao Chen\nL42@P1: V . Recalling that the Grassmanian manifold G(n, m) has dimension m(n − m), this\nL43@P1: is why we compare with it in the above estimates. Note that the upper bound in (b)\nL44@P1: is sharp, while the knowledge for the upper bounds in (a) and (c) are not complete,\nL45@P1: see Mattila [17, Chapter 5] for more details.\nL46@P1: Recently there has been a growing interest in studying finite field version of some\nL47@P1: classical problems arising from Euclidean spaces. For instance, there are finite field\nL48@P1: Kakeya sets (also called Besicovitch sets) [6], [9], [29], [30], there are finite field Erdős/\nL49@P1: Falconer distance problem [11], [27], etc.\nL50@P1: Motivated by the above works, we study the projections in vector spaces over\nL51@P1: finite fields. We show some notations first. Let Fp denote the finite field with\nL52@P1: p elements where p is prime, and F\nL53@P1: n\nL54@P1: p be the n-dimensional vector space over this\nL55@P1: field. The number of the m-dimensional linear subspaces of F\nL56@P1: n\nL57@P1: p\nL58@P1: is\nL59@P1: n\nL60@P1: m\nL61@P1: \u0001\nL62@P1: p\nL63@P1: which is\nL64@P1: called Gaussian coefficient, see [3], [13] for more details. Note that to obtain a m\u0002dimensional subspace, it is sufficient to choose m linear independent vectors. For\nL65@P1: the first vector we have p\nL66@P1: n − 1 choices from Fn\nL67@P1: p\nL68@P1: (except the zero vector); for the\nL69@P1: second vector we have p\nL70@P1: n − p choices to make that the second vector is independent\nL71@P1: to the first one; and so on. In the end we have (p\nL72@P1: n − 1)(pn − p)· · ·(pn − pm−1\nL73@P1: )\nL74@P1: amount of choices. Note that for each m-dimensional subspace, there are amount of\nL75@P1: (p\nL76@P1: m − 1)(pn − p)· · ·(pm − pm−1\nL77@P1: ) choices which generate (or span) the same subspace.\nL78@P1: It follows that (see [3, Theorem 6.3], [13] for more details)\nL79@P1: (1) \u0012\nL80@P1: n\nL81@P1: m\nL82@P1: \u0013\nL83@P1: p\nL84@P1: =\nL85@P1: (p\nL86@P1: n − 1)(pn − p)· · ·(pn − pm−1\nL87@P1: )\nL88@P1: (pm − 1)(pm − p)· · ·(pm − pm−1)\nL89@P1: .\nL90@P1: One interesting fact of Gaussian binomial coefficient is that\nL91@P1: \u0012\nL92@P1: n\nL93@P1: m\nL94@P1: \u0013\nL95@P1: p\nL96@P1: = (1 + o(p))p\nL97@P1: m(n−m)\nL98@P1: .\nL99@P1: The notation o(p) means that o(p) goes to zero as p goes to infinity. However, the\nL100@P1: following estimate is enough 